    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>'s second republic was declared on 27 April 1945 with the approval of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>, while most of the west and south were still under Nazi rule; the Federal Constitutional Law of 1920 was declared valid again on 1 May 1945.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-state-treaty">Austrian State Treaty</a> of 15 May 1955 ended ten years of Allied occupation; on 26 October 1955, with the last troops gone, parliament declared the country's perpetual neutrality, and the day is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>'s National Day.</li><li>After a referendum carried by two-thirds in 1994 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> on 1 January 1995, signed the Schengen Agreement the same year and adopted the euro in 1999; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> hosts the OSCE and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opec">OPEC</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> built a nuclear power station at Zwentendorf on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> after a unanimous vote of parliament in 1972, then voted 50.5% to 49.5% against nuclear power in a 1978 referendum; parliament banned it and the finished plant never produced electricity.</p></aside>
    </section>

