    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/karl-renner"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karl-renner.jpg" alt="Karl Renner" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/karl-renner">Karl Renner</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karl_Renner_1-E-901_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The republic declared in 1945 was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>'s second. The first, proclaimed as German-Austria in 1918 from the German-speaking lands of the fallen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy and renamed by the Treaty of Saint-Germain, lasted until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/engelbert-dollfuss">Engelbert Dollfuss</a> shut down parliament in 1933; the Austrian Civil War of February 1934 outlawed the Social Democrats, and on 13 March 1938 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anschluss">Anschluss</a> made Austria a part of Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Under Nazi rule Jewish property was seized, the pogrom of November 1938 struck <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> and other cities, and the Mauthausen camp and its sub-camps worked prisoners to death. Some 65,000 Jewish Austrians were murdered in the Holocaust, 140,000 had fled in 1938–39, and 260,000 Austrians died in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wehrmacht">Wehrmacht</a>.</p><p>Vienna fell to the Soviet offensive on 13 April 1945. On 27 April <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-renner">Karl Renner</a> and Adolf Schärf of the Socialists, Leopold Kunschak of the Christian Socials and Johann Koplenig of the Communists declared Austria's secession from the Third Reich and set up a provisional government under Renner with the approval of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>; the date is officially the birthday of the republic. On 1 May the constitution of 1920, abolished by Dollfuss in 1934, was declared valid again. The country was divided into four occupation zones under the Allied Commission for Austria, with the government in Vienna inside the Soviet zone, and was recognised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> in October 1945. It was a landlocked alpine land of 83,879 square kilometres and nine states.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Second Republic began with nationalisation: on 26 July 1946 parliament took about 70 mining and manufacturing companies into state hands. Many of the largest firms stayed nationalised until the 1980s, after which privatisation brought state holdings down to the levels of other European economies. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> built a highly industrialised social market economy in which labour organisations and the chambers of workers, business people and farmers weigh heavily, and it consistently ranks high in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gross-domestic-product">GDP</a> per capita, today the 13th highest in the world. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> has always been the main trading partner, which leaves Austria exposed to the German economy; at least 67% of imports come from other EU states, and membership from 1995 drew foreign investors seeking the single market and the economies to the east.</p><p>Austrian firms became consolidators in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a> after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a>, announcing 4,868 mergers and acquisitions worth 163 billion euros between 1995 and 2010. International tourism is the most important sector beside industry, almost 9% of GDP, with 20.8 million arrivals in 2007. More than half of the electricity comes from hydropower and renewables supply 62.89%. The population reached 9.22 million in April 2026; 22.3% were foreign-born in 2024, mostly from the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, Germany, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, and growth to 10.18 million by 2080 is expected from immigration. Catholics, 74% in 2001, were about 50% in 2023.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Second Republic has never fought a war, and its defence rests on the neutrality declared in 1955, by which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> will join no military alliance and allow no foreign bases on its soil. The Austrian Armed Forces, the Bundesheer, rely on conscription: men found fit at eighteen serve six months followed by an eight-year reserve obligation, and women have been able to become professional soldiers since 1998. The army has about 26,000 soldiers, some 12,000 of them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">conscripts</a>, organised in Joint Forces of land, air, international and special forces; as a landlocked country Austria has no navy. Defence spending was about 0.8% of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gross-domestic-product">GDP</a> in 2012. The president is nominal commander-in-chief, the minister of defence exercises command.</p><p>Within its neutrality Austria has a tradition of UN-led peacekeeping and humanitarian missions, with larger contingents in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a> and the all-volunteer Austrian Forces Disaster Relief Unit, able to deploy in ten hours. After the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> it reassessed its stance, granting overflight rights for the UN-sanctioned action against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> in 1991, joining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a>'s Partnership for Peace in 1995 only after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> had, and taking part in the EU's Common Foreign and Security Policy; the ban on foreign bases is the only part of the 1955 law still fully in force. The army helped the border guards against illegal crossings from the Eastern bloc frontier until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a> joined Schengen in 2008.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> is a federal, representative democratic republic of nine states under the Federal Constitutional Law of 1920, amended in 1929 and re-enacted on 1 May 1945. The president, directly elected with a run-off, is head of state and selects the chancellor, who forms a government on the strength of the Nationalrat; that chamber of 183 seats is elected every five years by citizens over sixteen, the voting age lowered in 2007, and can dismiss the government by a vote of no confidence. The Constitutional Court invalidates laws that breach the constitution, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/court-of-justice-of-the-european-union">European Court of Justice</a> has overruled Austrian decisions since 1995, and the European Convention on Human Rights is part of the constitution.</p><p>Politics was long governed by Proporz: important posts were shared between the Social Democratic Party (SPÖ) and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-peoples-party">Austrian People's Party</a> (ÖVP), and the chambers of interest groups were consulted on nearly all legislation. Single-party governments ruled only in 1966–1970 (ÖVP) and 1970–1983 (SPÖ); otherwise grand coalitions or small coalitions with a third party held office. From 2006 both big parties weakened as Jörg Haider's right-wing parties and later the Greens and NEOS grew; Sebastian Kurz governed with the Freedom Party from December 2017 and with the Greens from January 2020 until his resignation in a corruption scandal in October 2021, followed by Alexander Schallenberg, Karl Nehammer and, after the 2024 election, Christian Stocker.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/vienna"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vienna.jpg" alt="Vienna" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schoenbrunn_philharmoniker_2012.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Superbass, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Neutral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> made itself a meeting place. It joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> in 1955, is a founding member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oecd">OECD</a> and Interpol, and hosts the Organization for Security and Co-operation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opec">Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, capital of the former multi-ethnic Habsburg Empire, kept its role as the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a> with the strongest ties east and south, and after the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> Austrian banks and companies became leading investors in the former Eastern bloc. Membership of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> from 1995, the Schengen Agreement and the euro bound the country into the west, while the SPÖ and ÖVP still argue over whether neutrality or closer European defence, even <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a>, should define it.</p><p>The republic also had to face its past. In 1992 Chancellor Franz Vranitzky officially acknowledged that thousands of Austrians had taken part in serious Nazi crimes. The Carinthian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and Hungarians of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgenland">Burgenland</a> received minority rights under the State Treaty of 1955, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Roma</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinti">Sinti</a> were recognised in 1994. The loss of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-tyrol">South Tyrol</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> stayed a grievance before Italy granted the region wide autonomy. Austria's cultural weight continued: Herbert von Karajan led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> Philharmonic for 35 years, the quantum physicists Anton Zeilinger and Peter Zoller followed Schrödinger and Pauli, and the country ranked 17th in the Global Innovation Index in 2024.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Into the Present</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Second Republic's recent decades have been marked by the loosening of its two-party order. In the 2008 election the SPÖ fell to 29.3% and the ÖVP to 26.0%, while the Freedom Party and Jörg Haider's Alliance for the Future of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> together approached 20%. The grand coalition broke in 2017, and Sebastian Kurz's ÖVP took 31.5% and governed with the Freedom Party until the Ibiza corruption scandal forced new elections in 2019; his coalition with the Greens, sworn in on 7 January 2020, lost him to another corruption scandal in October 2021, and Austria had three conservative chancellors in two months. After the election of 29 September 2024 talks between ÖVP, SPÖ and NEOS first collapsed, Karl Nehammer resigned, an ÖVP–FPÖ attempt failed, and the three parties finally formed the Stocker government.</p><p>The economy weathered the 2008 crisis with scars: the government bought the Hypo Alpe-Adria-Bank for one euro in 2009, and in 2014 Chancellor Werner Faymann warned that its failure would compare with the Creditanstalt collapse of 1931. Today the republic faces an ageing society, with an average age of 44.5 years in 2020, and a climate that has warmed almost 2 °C since 1880, bringing more heat waves, floods and landslides and shrinking glaciers and snow. It remains among the most prosperous and peaceful states in the world, neutral by law, a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> and host to international organisations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Second Austrian Republic was declared on 27 April 1945 as the Third Reich collapsed, regained full independence by the State Treaty of 1955 and declared perpetual neutrality that October. It grew into one of the world's richest states, joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> in 1995 and the euro in 1999, and governs nine federal states from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, host of the OSCE and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opec">OPEC</a>.</em></p>

